Would anyone from DEC be willing to comment on the official word about
OSF/1 on DEC's MIPS boxes?  Is it definitely true that it won't happen,
or was this yet another big misunderstanding, or ...

Since the DN&R article which quoted Strecker, I've seen nothing from any
DEC source commenting on it (either to say "yeah, it's true, sorry" or to
say "no, no, he didn't really mean it", or whatever) anywhere, either in
the trade rags, or on the net.

I had assumed that this meant that the comment as reported was DEC's final
official word on the topic.  But someone I know who works for DEC Canada
told me that as recently as last Friday, *they* were being told (by some
allegedly Big Cheese down in wherever-it-is that the Big Cheeses hang out)
that MIPS OSF/1 *would* happen, but that it would be delayed a few months.

If in fact a final decision has been made not to do it (and *really* final,
not like the "we will; we won't; no, he didn't mean that -- we will" nonsense
of a while back), I'm astounded that DEC's management doesn't realize how bad
they'll look to their customers (both current and potential).  Either they do
realize it, or they don't.  If they do, it means they don't care -- which is
worrisome.  And if they don't realize, they're amazingly out of touch with
reality, which is equally worrisome.

Strecker is probably correct that people are moving (or will be moving) toward
Alpha, but this still begs the question of what to do about customers who have
a bunch of DEC MIPS systems, want to hold onto them, want to buy Alphas, and
would like to have the same flavour of UNIX on all their machines.  Ultrix
and OSF/1 have a lot in common, to be sure, but there are enough differences
that I can understand that people would strongly prefer to have OSF/1 on both.

The issue doesn't affect our group directly, as we have no DEC MIPS systems,
and are buying Alphas.  However, we were thinking of getting some MIPS boxes
from DEC as we start phasing out our low-end workstations in favour of some
better/faster hardware.  Not wanting to learn yet another variant of UNIX,
the unavailability of MIPS OSF/1 would make a purchase of that sort far less
likely.

Of course, the Alpha PCs may be so cheap and so fast that we wouldn't want
to buy a MIPS box from DEC anyway.  But that remains to be seen.  And in
any case, it's no consolation for the poor sod who already has MIPS boxes,
whose decision to buy them may have been based on Digital's promise that
OSF/1 would be available, and who is now stuck.

I suppose one way of pacifying the disgruntled customers would be to offer
a *huge* tradein on MIPS boxes toward purchase of Alpha systems.  (And by
huge, I mean something similar to what Sun was doing for a while:  At one
point they were offering nearly $10K (Canadian $) off the list price of a
Sparc-2 if you traded in an old diskless Sun-3/50.  (We just happened to be
buying some Sparcs anyway, so we went out and bought some used Sun3s for
about $700 each.  Such a deal! :-)

However, the whole issue of corporate credibility comes into play.  After
a sequence of "yes" -- "no" -- "yes" -- and now "no" again, how can DEC's
customers possibly believe *anything* DEC says?

And speaking of broken promises, it's my understanding that there are people
whose decision to buy MIPS boxes from DEC was influenced by the promise that
an R4000 upgrade would be available as well.  Now it appears that this, too,
may be a pipe dream.

Finally, if in fact top management has decided to kill MIPS OSF/1, it means
that Digital is (or at least was, as recently as a week ago) lying to its
own employees.

Is it any surprise that Digital seems to have very little credibility with
the people it hopes will buy its products?

