This isn't a direct response, but an observation:

DEC has handled this problem in the one way guaranteed to piss off the most
users.

It wouldn't have been so bad if they announced "Alpha is so great that we've
realized the MIPS platform is not strategic for us, so we're not going to port
OSF and will be in maintenance mode on Ultrix/RISC for the next couple of
years", and it would have been pretty satisfactory to most people if they'd
complemented that with *really good* trade-in terms on Alphas.  That would have
come pretty close to keeping faith with the customers, would have quickly upped
the Alpha installed base, and maybe even generated a little revenue.

Agreeing to do OSF after all seemed to show that DEC would listen to customers
if they yelled really loudly, which was better than nothing.

To flip-flop back to the first position, after presumably selling some
MIPS-based systems to people who might not have bought them without an OSF
upgrade path irritates the preexisting customers (who feel that they were lied
to in order to pacify them), the customers who bought MIPS after the
doing-OSF-after-all announcement (who feel they've been defrauded), and plenty
of people who had no direct involvement in this fiasco but who will now be
warier about making any long-term plans that involve DEC.

DEC isn't the only company that does this kind of thing -- I'm under the 
impression that the Apollo user community isn't too happy about a forced
conversion to HP architectures -- but handling it this way was just
phenomenally maladroit.

Sorry for the absence of technical comment in this post - I'll try not to do it
again.

-- 

