In article <WARREN.93Feb24140156@roma.atmos.washington.edu> warren@atmos.washington.edu (David Warren) writes:
>Having tried the KAP preprocessors, I'm not convinced that  I'll use
>them. They take a very long time to run and seem to die on some very
>legitimate fortran and c code. They also do not seem to make much of a
>difference for most of the code I've run through them. Also as the
>documentation points out they decrease the number of significant
>digits that you can have.
>--

My experience is similar.  It is quite good at finding loops and so forth
to parallelize automagically.  However, the codes I tried needed to have
the loops *created* first, then parallelized.  This is the big problem.
For fun and games, I dropped my original, pure fortran, chess program (Cray
Blitz) into KAP on a 8 processor YMP and it ran 1.01 times faster.  KAP
found lots of loops in the *move input* routine to parallelize, but could
do *nothing* with the basic search as there were no loops that were
parallelizable.  I spent years re-doing the search to create parallel
processing potential and didn't really expect KAP to do much.  It did churn
for a very long time on the code (>20,000 lines of fortran).  Now, if it
only knew how to re-write the code first, then do the dependency graph
analysis, it might make some headway.....

Note:  this is *not* a flame for KAP or Ken and group, just an opinion that
the problem is very much harder for lots of codes than one might think.

Bob Hyatt


