In article <24FEB199314271061@mary.fordham.edu>, areid@mary.fordham.edu (Darin Reid) writes:
> Will VMS suuport different terminals (actually a bigger variety is
> what I mean) in the near future?  I just bought a Televideo955, and
> I'm having a terrible time using it for my VMS sys.  I'm forced to use
> Gnu Emacs (which I'm still learning), instead of TPU, because Emacs
> has the "EMACS_TERM" logical, TPU has no equivalent...

I think it is highly unlikely.  Five or six years ago or so, when
there were lots more different kinds of terminals on the market, it
would have made sense.  Nowadays, almost all async terminals sold
are either ANSI terminals or have an ANSI mode.  Even HP terminals
can now be set up to look almost exactly like VT terminals.

If the Televideo doesn't have an ANSI mode (I know that some did 
but I don't remember about the 955 specifically), then you will
pretty much have to lump it.  There are some things you can do to
reduce the problems:

    1)	Use the SYS$SYSTEM:TERMTABLE.TXT file to rebuild the SMG
	terminal database file, SYS$SYSTEM:TERMTABLE.EXE.  This will
	allow the use of a number of utilities that use SMG.  (But
	unfortunately the editors distributed by DEC, EDT and TPU,
	don't get their escape sequences from TERMTABLE :-( - they
	are hardwired in the code).

    2)	Find an editor that can be customized for the terminal type
	or that uses the SMG terminal database TERMTABLE.EXE to define
	its escape sequences.  (There have also been a few editors
	floating around in different places -- FTP sites and DECUS --
	that use SMG directly.  This is of course also a possibility).
	Since you have Gnu Emacs, this may solve the problem unless
	you don't like Emacs and want to find a different editor.  I
	remember using a vi clone at one point on an HP terminal, but
	I no longer have it (I don't like vi, and just used it because
	I was writing a lot of graphics code for the terminal & it was
	easier to write it on the terminal I'd be displaying it on).

    3)	You might want to edit the SYS$EXAMPLES:SYSGTTSTR.MSG file
	and install it with SYSGEN to change the terminal driver's
	echo strings for ^Y, ^Z, etc.  This is pretty cosmetic though.

    4)	If there are any old programs around that still use the SCR$
	screen library rather than the SMG$ screen library, I don't
	think that the SCR$ library will use the SMG$ escape sequences
	on non-DEC terminals unless you create a SCRFT.EXE module in
	SYS$SHARE:.  A sample version of SCRFT.MAR is in SYS$EXAMPLES.
	The SCR$ library is documented in the Obsolete Features manual.

	(BTW, are there any standard VMS utilities that still use the
	SCR$ library?  There used to be a few, but most of them got
	converted to SMG$ over the years, & there are no longer any
	non-ANSI terminals around here so the issue pretty much went
	away & I don't know what the current state of affairs is).

Good luck - 

Bruce C. Wright


