pavlov@niktow.canisius.edu (Greg Pavlov) writes:
>
>  SGI has always seemed to get better (sometimes MUCH better) systems perfor-
>  mance from a given MIPS processor than DEC.  Don't know if it's the soft-
>  ware, better support hardware, or a combination of both .....

	Sometimes it's as simple as running under the latest/greatest version
of the compiler. I was once involved in benchmarking a DECsystem500/240
against an SGI Indigo. By clock and processor speeds, the 5000/240 should
have been a tad under twice as fast. First time we ran the benchmark the
SGI was 6 times faster. A little pixiefying identified malloc() as the
culprit and once that was replaced with the BSD version, the systems ran
with the 5000/240 showing a slight edge.

	Further experiments with pixie indicated that the 5000/240 was
executing an average of 1.7 instructions for every 1 that the SGI did.
This was more or less across the board, including in the C support
libraries. A simple call to fprintf() on the SGI [an isolated case I
measured] used almost half as many instructions. Pretty depressing.
The DECsystems's compiler was a rev behind the SGI machine - I don't
know if that was the sole cause of the difference. The net effect
was that the DECsystem was a faster box but was wasting most of its
energy.

	IMHO, too many vendors neglect software tuning in favor of
just making faster boxes. Investing money in optimising the C library
pays off across the entire product line. Most vendors focus on
kernel optimizations, which is good, but there's still a lot of
room for improvement! I once suggested building a system with some
form of profiling support for all C library routines. You count the
number of cycles system-wide that are spent in a given routine, and
let the machine run under normal workload for a month or two. Then
identify the top functions in cycle usage overall and rework them
or tune the assembler, or whatever. If my experience in the benchmark
is any indication, a well-tuned c library is worth a clock speedup. ;)
Pyramid, back in the 9020 days, used to do this, and would re-write
the "hot" C library and FORTRAN library routines in assembler.

mjr.


