In article <1993Feb23.191504.1608@lambda.msfc.nasa.gov>, thigpen@lambda.msfc.nasa.gov (Keith Thigpen) writes:
|>I'm trying to get a DECwindows application to display on a remote xwindows
|>system using the 'set display /create /node=128.158.33.185:0' command. I've 
|>also tried the node=sorcerer.msfc.nasa.gov but when I try to run the app, 
|>it crashes with the following msg:
|>
|>X Toolkit Error: Can't Open display
|>%DWT-F-DWTABORT, xtoolkit fatal error
|>%TRACE-F-TRACEBACK, symbolic stack dump follows
|>module name     routine name                     line       rel PC    abs PC

Looks to me like you've forgotten to specify a transport. The SET DISPLAY
command assumes you want the DECNET transport unless you say otherwise,
and "128.158.33.185" doesn't look like a DECnet host name to me.

Try a command like:

	$ SET DISPLAY /CREATE /NODE=128.158.33.185 /TRANSPORT=trans

where 'trans' is:

	WINTCP for Wollongong WIN/TCP
	TCPIP  for DEC UCX, TGV Multinet, or
	       WIN/TCP's UCX Emulation mode
	CMUTEK for CMU-Tek (not sure 'bout this one)

BTW, I assume the ':0' part of the address is intended to specify the
server number. That is specified using the /SERVER=nn qualifier, and
defaults to zero.

|>VMS help says the set display command is only used to talk to one DECwindows
|>host to another. Is there any way that I can display on an X Windows system?
|>Anyone have any ideas?

DEC always says their stuff only talks to their stuff. I believe this is
because that's all they will officially support, even if it actually will
work with other systems.

--
Reece R. Pollack
Senior Software Engineer
The Wollongong Group, Inc.


