As of VXT software version 1.2, this can't be done. You can hide the VXT Terminal
Manager window so that it isn't visible (and I think you can keep it from appearing)
so that the inquisitive minds don't get access to it.


|>	- have you found any way out of having XDM reboot the terminal
|>	  at the end of each session, or if you've managed to make the
|>	  terminal automatically reboot, without someone having to hit
|>	  RETURN by hand.
|>

VXT software version 1.2 has 3 options for dealing with the reset server that
xdm transmits when ending a session:

        1) Ignore the reset. This allows any local clients that you have
           started to continue. It also may not kill all clients that were
           started on your system when you had the xdm session active.
        2) Reset the terminal. This does not reboot the terminal, instead
           it does what you'd expect a server reset to do: kills all open windows 
           and local clients, and re-starts the local terminal manager and auto-
           started clients. Takes about 10 seconds from logout to
           the re-appearance of the terminal manager. And you don't have to hit
           return.
           To use xdm on VXT, I set up an autostarted ip X session to the host
           of my choice, then configured my VXT for this option. When the VXT
           boots, I automatically get an xdm login box. If you want, you can hide
           the terminal manager.
        3) Reboot the terminal. Ugly. The default action before VXT 1.2 software.
           Requires you to hit the RETURN.

Check the VXT software documentation for the exact location of these options, 
but if memory serves me right, they're in the terminal configuration menus. 

VXT 1.2 software is essential for any of this to work. Previous versions were, uh,
shall we say, less than robust in this regard.

