In article <C2ynBu.AsI@stl.dk>, leo@stl.dk (Leif Olsen) writes:
> In the "DECstation 5000/240 Technical Overwiev" it is stated that
> the HX-controller can operate in three modes:
> 
>    1280x1024
>    1024x864
>    1024x768
> 
> For test purposes, we'd like to - temporarily - setup the HX in
> 1024x864 mode in order to simulate the resolution of a CX controller.
> 
> Q: How can this be achived ?
> 
>    Do we have to change some jumpers on the HX-board ?
>    Do the Xws server need some additional options ?
>    Do we need to rebuild the kernel ?
>    Can we use the same monitor (VRT-19 - 66/72 HZ) ?
> 
The HX display parameters are set by two crystals on the board,
a single jumper and a look up table for the video parameters in ROM.


The jumper selects which crystal is used.  From the crystal
frequency, the board figures out what the video parameters ought to be
(from the on board ROM at power up time) and sets the video parameters
(by use of a counter, and the code in the ROM executed at powerup).
All variants support 1280x1024x72HZ.

So if the jumper is on (or maybe off, I don't have a spec in front of me),
the other crystal is selected, and therefore the video parameters set to
some other resolution and video parameters.  So what crystal is in the second
position determines the alternate resolution/frame rate/video parameters.

Actually, a wide variety of resolutions, frame rates, and video parameters
are in the ROM, all waiting to be set by the correct frequency crystal;
Digital has only tested the monitors and frame resolutions we offer, however.

So if you have the correct variant board, all  you need to do is play with
the jumper; if not, you'll have to get the correct crystal (which is actually
a bit of a chore, as the frequency is high enough that vanilla crystals can't
be used, so just going to your junk pile probably won't work.  You better
order the same model crystals we use.).

You don't need to set anything in either the kernel or the X server.

At boot time, Ultrix and/or OSF looks at the board and figures out the
resolution it is running at, which is reflected at the driver interface
to the server, and the server does the right thing from there.  So no reconfiguration
of software, either kernel or server is required on your part to run the 
HX at whatever resolution you like.

Here is a table of monitors and video the ROM knows about; note that only the DEC monitors 
have been tested; you are on your own with non-Digital monitors.

The DEC Std listing is 1024x864x60 HZ, and corresponds to the CX; monitors
used at this resolution and frame rate include the VR260, VR262, VR290, VR297.
So you need a 69.20MHZ crystal, if your board isn't the version which has it
on it in the first place.



