I have heard two things -- the educational discount of 7% may
be vanishing and that there will be a $50 shipping charge for
each machine.  

If this is correct, DEC's plan to remain competitive with DELL
on pricing is in serious problems.  And if they don't remain
competitive in price here, they will have again flip flopped
on a statement of direction.  If the pricing was to try to get
people in the habit of buying DEC for the desktop in preparation
for future Alpha PCs, they are going to get people to kick that
habit before it even gets started.

And if the argument is going to be that their machines are so
much better and everyone _should_ buy them even though they
will cost more, they need to check recent history at the kind
of loyalty IBM saw when they tried that with microchannel PS/2
machines.  And IBM was wallowing in customer loyalty at the time.

Anyway, I hope the information that sparked this note is wrong.
And if it is correct, perhaps the loss of the 7% educational
discount will be coupled by at least that kind of overall
price drop for commercial, educational, and private customers.

Michael Crowley


