>already exists) and for comp.os.rsx.

The newsgroup "comp.sys.rsts" is in the "inet" category, which implies
(or at least it used to) that the group is not well distributed in the
store-and-forward UUCP world, although many Internet sites do carry it.

In the "vmsnet" hierarchy, which is guided by a group operating under
DECUS, there is the newsgroup "vmsnet.pdp-11"--a forum for discussion
of all issues pertaining to DEC's famous 16-bit minicomputer.  The
messages in the group are bi-directionally gatewayed to the mailing list
"Info-PDP11".

This month, the traffic in vmsnet.pdp-11 has concerned mostly hardware,
but discussion of RSX or RT-11 (or even RSTS) is always welcome.

The "vmsnet" groups have very good distribution; any site that wants a
feed of any part of the "vmsnet" hierarchy should be able to obtain one.
If your local newsfeeds lack vmsnet, contact "tp@mccall.com".

Failing that, you can send a query to Info-PDP11-Request@transarc.com
and ask to get the messages by e-mail.

I have never seen "comp.sys.pdp8".  Perhaps this is a group that exists
only at United Kingdom sites.  However, I used to subscribe to the
\mailing list/ for the DEC PDP-8.  A description of this follows:

pdp8-lovers
    Contact: pdp8-lovers-request@mc.lcs.mit.edu  (Robert E. Seastrom)

    Purpose: This mailing list exists in order to facilitate
    communication and cooperation between owners of vintage DEC
    computers, specifically, but not limited to, the PDP-8 series of
    minicomputers.  Discussions of all manner of hardware, software,
    programming techniques are invited.  Ownership of an 'antique'
    computer is not required for membership, but flames from people
    who feel that anything that is not cutting edge technology is
    worthless are discouraged.

    (An archive of this list's traffic is kept on the MIT systems.)

(I could also tell you about a mailing list for discussion of Stratus
and IBM System/88 computers, but I'll spare you the gory details :-)

