In article <1ltpdg$eq4@fnnews.fnal.gov>, lauri@elwing.fnal.gov writes...
>William M. Jackson writes:
>
>>The FDDI module is not supported in the Model 60 due to lack of high speed
>>DMA buffering in the Model 60 implementation.  The Model 90 does implement this
>>buffering and thus can support the FDDI controller.  These are supported in
>>VMS/VAX V5.5-2
>
>Note: our site just found out (painfully) that the DEFZA has DMA for
>received packets, but *NOT* for transmitted packets.  In our configuration
>(VAXstation 4000 model 90 with optional DEC FDDI controller 700) this
>implied that the interrupt stack usage was 80-85 percent, just to service
>the FDDI controller.  Ouch.  (By comparison, the Ethernet controller
>averaged around 12 percent interrupt stack time).

    Interesting.  We have a pair of DEFZA controllers connected to
4000 model 90s and have found that when you try to transmit a lot of
data fast, they roll over and die (reset).  The result of these resets
is that the controller bandwidth is worse than Ethernet, and we are
recommending to our customer that they don't bother trying these
boards.  If you "slow down" MultiNet, they work.  Colorado Support
wasn't interested in the problem since it involved non-DEC software.

    I wonder if the problem isn't that spending so much time on the
interrupt stack ends up blocking bus interrupts for longer than the
controller can handle.

    The DEMFA on the other hand is a good, solid controller.

							    Ken


