>
I've installed 3 different types of 3rd party SCSI disk drives onto my
5000/133 and 5000/240 systems and have had no problems.  In fact, I don't
have any DEC drives at all.  One type is the Micropolis 1520 (formats to
1.7 GB), which I had some problems with one of the drives at first, but
no more once the vendor replaced it.  I also have a 3-1/2" form factor
Micropolis model 2112 that formats to 1 GB installed inside the DS/133
whose only annoying problem is a high pitched whine.  Finally, I have a
Seagate WREN 7 drive (1GB formatted) also attached to the DS/133.  The only
problem I've had was with the default disk parameters that ULTRIX uses
when you format the drives.  I don't fully understand the logic behind
them, but I retuned the parameters and got MUCH better performance.
Oh yeah, the Micropolis drives used zone bit recording so I couldn't
specify a real sectors per track parameter so I had to fake one.  This,
combined with the funny default disk parameters probably caused the
disks to really crawl.

Now, about the problems you're having:  are you running very long cables?
Single ended SCSI is supposed to allow up to 20 feet of total cable length
end to end, but I have seen some controllers wimp out at less than 10.
Other than that, have you made sure that the bus is terminated properly
on both ends?  The internal disk controller on DS5000 is terminated on
the inside.  The other end must be terminated at the last device, and only
once.  A lot of drives come with terminators installed in the drive already,
and if you install the drive in the middle of the chain, or at the end with
an external terminator with its internal terminator, you will cause problems
for a lot of devices in the chain.

eyc


