In article <1993Feb18.035903.26853@fantom.agchem.redwater.iol.ca>, p45dpmg@fantom.agchem.redwater.iol.ca (Pete Graw) writes:
> I would like to filter out interactive DECnet traffic (i.e. CTERM, or object
> 42) across a remote bridged link. Is it possible to tell file transfer traffic
> apart from interactive traffic via the contents of an ethernet packet? How?
> -- 
> --------------------------------------------------
> Peter Graw (p45dpmg@iol.ca)
> Imperial Oil - Chemicals Division
> Information Management
-- 

DEC has a software product that will do this.  It is called the DECnet
Security Gateway.  The Security Gateway has the ability to audit and filter
DECnet traffic based upon source/destination node, object type (i.e. CTERM
or other objects), source username, and time.

The security gateway software sits on a routing node and looks at packets
passing though the node.  It makes a determination if the connect request
or reconnect request packet should be allowed to connect based on the rules
set in the database.  If so, the packet is passed along.  Otherwise - no
connection.

If you choose to use this software, you will need to make sure your DECnet
topology is such that all packets you want to filter pass through the node
running the Security Gateway.

