In article <ZHAO.93Feb12105506@pylos.nmsu.edu>, zhao@nmsu.edu (Z. Zhao) writes:
>
>If I made a mistake of deleting a useful device driver, how could I
>recover it. Should I follow the steps in manuel about how to install a
>new device driver: do configuration, build kernel, .... This is the
>first time for me to handle device drivers, so I am very confused.
>
Are you proposing to delete a kernel or the object file(s) for the
device driver of interest.  If you delete the object file(s) then
you'll have lost the ability to build a new kernel with that
device driver in it (and possibly build a new kernel at all).

If you deleted a device driver distributed as part of the ULTRIX
kit you can get it off the distribution, though it will take some
work.  The supported device drivers are the BIN subset.  The unsupported
device drivers appear to be in the UNSUPPORTED BASE subset.  These
are compress tar archives.  If you can get the subset file off the
distribution media, it's simple matter to uncompress and use tar(1)
to extract the file.

The ULTRIX FAQ (available on gatekeeper.dec.com) has information
about the structure of the distribution media.  For CDROM it is
easy; just mount it.

For 3rd party or layered product device drivers, it will depend
on the structure of the distribution.

