Did the people who wrote this considered for a moment they tried
a Beta version of a product ? And I really don't know if they tried
it at all....

I won't comment on the entire text ( I am not paid to make such
advanced studies ... ), but here are some points :



> Configurations need 16Mb or more to achieve acceptable performance

Microsoft puts 8 Mb for final version. I am running NT on an 8Mb 
PC now, and I aggree it is not blazing speed, but look again :
which operating system with all the features of NT runs at lightening
speed with 8 Mb ?

>  It can support
>multiple clients but only one actual user.  Users hell-bent on
>transaction processing, the key downsizing element, had better look
>elsewhere.  

If you are considering TP client, you are talking about the
end user desktop. Why on earth would you work with 2 people on the
same desktop PC at the same time ?
If you are considering TP server, why would you have multiple people
logged in to the server ? Any TP monitor creates some processes where
clients can attach to do transactions, which can be done very well on NT
via RPC's or other mechanisms.
If you want multi user log in, it's available from 3th party now.

> File transfer functions, for instance, are not supported.

Bizar, I am using ftp from my standard NT Beta release all the time,
even over the internet.

> Multinationals meanwhile are going to find NT a bit parochial

NT supports full Unicode 16 bit strings for internationalization.

>On the graphical user interface side, a user's ability to manipulate system objects
>from the desktop is limited in NT as are drag-and-drop capabilities, a
>definite drawback.

Did they ever tried a desktop manager for Windows ?

> those relying on Dynamically Linked Libraries
> written for the 16-bit Windows interface will have to be converted to
> the Win32 API to be implemented.

No. 16 bit Windows application run on the NT system without convertion
using the VDM (Virtual DOS Machine). In fact, a VDM machine is created
for all 16 bit Windows applications. This group of applications is
scheduled as on Windows 3.1 ( no preemption in this group ). However,
the VDM itself is scheduled on a preemtive basis with the rest of the
applications running on NT ( WIN32, POSIX, ... ).



>  NT is not as easy to install as Windows.

Is it ? I got it up and running in 45 minutes from CD-rom, and a diskette
installation is available. By the way, the 45 minutes included TCP/IP
and LAN Manager services.

>Worst, perhaps, Microsoft has neither provided nor
>announced support for Network File System or any other native Unix
>distributed file system.

PC-NFS is available right now from 3th party.


> Moreover, NT provides no native support for OSI, X25, SNA or DECNet

As does nearly every other system in the world I guess.


What I do aggree is that the POSIX side is weak. In principal an X Windows
application basicly will have to be rewritten for use on NT if it wants
to keep a graphical user interface. But that's a developers burden, not
an end user burden...and maybe unfortunately for developers but the end user
pays their salaries...
If you want to use POSIX, you are limited to console mode. In fact, the
POSIX subsystem uses the WIN32 subsystem only for console I/O.
If however you want a nice shell with some favorite Ux goodies,
I am running here Hamilton C Shell on my NT machine and I like it.


Dominique Dierick

Open Systems Support Center,
Graphics Specialist
Brussels


