In article <C1y2s5.LuI@news2.cis.umn.edu>, gary@umnstat.stat.umn.edu (Gary Oehlert) writes:
|>
|>I have a DEC RZ24 (200 Meg Conner drive, SCSI) that I am trying to use
|>in another system (a Mac).  I'm not getting to first base because the
|>drive doesn't spin-up or wake-up or whatever when given power.  It
|>is waiting for some command from the host.
|>

This is due to the design of the VAXstation and DECstation systems.  We had 
wanted to get as much storage into those boxes with as little power as 
possible, so we disabled spinup on power up, thus allowing the software to 
sequence the drives (and use less power)

In non VMS or Unix applications (like PC's and Macs) you have to enable 
spinup on power up.  This is stored in the firmware on the drive, which 
can be written several ways.

1:	Use Silverlining (LaCie) on your Macintosh.  It'll set the
	spinup on power up bit and all will be well

2:	Use a DECstation and the SCSI diagnostics

3:	Use a VAXstation and the service mode diagnostics.  There is a
	procedure to do this, however you need the self-test "spoons" 
	and some "magic"  Certainly not the easiest way to do things

4:	use a DOS PC and the a  shareware program called SPINUP (or 
	something like that)  

Obviously, the easiest way for you to do it is to use Silverlining, since
you're trying to use a Macintosh anyway.   


