mmisra@slate.mines.colorado.edu (MISRA MANAVENDRA ) writes:
|> Hi!  I have a DECstation and I find that each time I want to do an
|> su root, it comes back with a:
|> 
|> getwd: Permission denied -- can't start new shell
|> 
|> error.  How can I rectify this?  Thanks.

One likely cause of this is that you're running su in a directory that's
on an NFS filesystem exported without root equivalency (-r=0), and the
directory is non-world-readable and/or non-world-executable.  In this
case, root doesn't have privilege to read the directory it's in, and
therefore getwd(3) fails, and therefore root's shell refuses to start.

Easiest workaround: cd to / (or some other world-readable and/or local
directory) before running su root.

Some shells deal with this situation better than others.  tcsh, for example,
will always chdir("/") itself if getwd() fails.

-Shawn Mamros
E-mail to: mamros@athena.lkg.dec.com


