bleau@umdsp.umd.edu wrote:

+ I posted the note/question about a problem with a Tahiti-II drive a
+ few days ago.  Well, I think I have a handle on it.  I was speaking with Randy
+ Douglas, sales representative for Mesa Integrated Systems (800-487-7911), about
+ another topic, and I mentioned the Tahiti-II problem.  Turns out he knew all
+ about it.  There is, I'm told, a problem/bug with the DEC software that
+ controls SCSI devices, which causes a timeout when used with optical drives. 
+ I'm unclear if this is a system firmware or a VMS device driver problem, but I
+ suspect it is a driver problem, since one of the proposed solutions (see below)
+ is a software one. Randy outlined two solutions for me.

	I'm probably getting in over my head here, but I'd say the
problem is not with VMS from the info you've described above, even
though one fix isa VMS driver fix. According to the SCSI standard,
when there's a request on the drive that requires it to spin up for
the first time, it's supposed to come back with a responce as soon
as possible saying "I'm here", spin the drive up, then say "OK, I'm
ready".
	The problem is that a lot of drives don't do anything until
they have completed spinup. As this can take a while, systems
sticking to the SCSI specs can have decided in the meantime (due to
the lack of immediate responce) that there's nothing there at that
SCSI ID.
	The software in this case is simply to increase the timeout.
So the software fix is easy, but it's because the drive's firmware
is not up to spec.
	Of course, SCSI is not my strong point so I may be way out
of line here. Anyone else care to comment?

	Andrew.

