In article <1993Jan15.142835.1014@dragon.com>, cts@dragon.com writes:
> I have an application which is being moved from a PDP11/44 (may it rest in
> peace real soon now) to a MicroVAX system (actually, its one of the redundant
> Mira systems).  Anyway, this application used to use a DR11-W on the PDP
> side;  this is being changed to a DRV11-W.  
> 
This should not pose problem.  The DRV11-W is the correct Q-BUS equivelant for
a DR11-W.

> The driver used for this device is custom;  it was started on by an individual
> who then left the company;  I picked it up;  I should have thrown it out 
> and started from scratch... however.
> 

Does it appear to you the customized device driver was written from scratch, or
was the XADRIVER.MAR in SYS$EXAMPLES used as a starting point?  XADRIVER does a
pretty good job at being a straight forward QIO-driven DMA device driver.

> 
> At best, we're seeing about 25 I/Os per second (from the user program point
> of view) out of this device.  Changing the packet size doesn't appear to 
> make a lot of difference;  the PDP seems to be able to do around 80 I/Os
> per second (this could include the double I/O);  in any case, it's 
> not fast enough. 
> 
I've been able to get a 250 word structure cycled in and out of memory at a
rate of 50 HZ (20ms) with the stock XADRIVER running on a MicroVAX 3600 (rated
at 2.7 VUPS).  This is 100 I/O operations per second. 

> Any thoughts, suggestions, or comments;  any hints on the DRV11-W 
> would be appreciated.
> 
Take a look at SYS$EXAMPLES:XADRIVER.MAR to see if what you have is close. 
Also, get a copy of Digital's Realtime User's Guide (dec order number
AA-PE1WA-TE).  This book has a very good explanation of ways to tune VMS to
improve realtime performance, and how to reduce system overhead in device i/o.
You should be able to get it either through your local Sales Office or from one
of the Digital 800 order numbers like Dec-Direct.

I would consider the book mandatory reading for anyone serious about doing
realtime work on a VAX.

> Also, in the same project, we will be using a DRV11-J to create an 
> interrupt (from a button push on a control pannel).  This interrupt 
> needs to be handled, and cause an output to the above DRV11-W in
> 4/30's of a second or less.  Is this a reasonable thing to achieve
> on a Vax?
> 

You should be able to do this if you are careful with your process priorities.

Also, does your VAX have to be running VAX/VMS? Or can it be running VAXeln for
the realtime kernel?  If so, you would have a whole new dimension to what you
can do with the realtime application.  The Realtime User's Guide also has a
very nice overview of VAXeln.

