DEC just happens to have a huge stockpile of VMS applications laying
about.  Now, just for fun, imagine that they actually want a return on
their investment for developing this software.

Here's one (easy) way to do it.

1.  Ship Alpha PC's with 
    A.  Wads of RAM (expandable with SIMMs - not DEC only boards)
    B.  At least one SCSI-2 port, a serial port, and a parallel port.
    C.  An ethernet port (with adapters for different formats, like a Quadra) 
    D.  A big internal hard drive (at least 400M).
    E.  A built in CD-ROM drive.
    F.  A PC/Mac compatible floppy drive.
    G.  Either built in high resolution graphics, or a slot for a
        "standard" PC graphics card.
    H.  A good keyboard/mouse/monitor.
    I.  A decent speaker/audio pathway.
    J.  A killer price (how does $3000 grab you)?
    K.  Preloaded OVMS/DecWindows/Motif/SoftDOS/Desktop Manager (plus ?)
    L.  ID registers (yuck, but it's better than Dongles and keys)

2.  Every 3 months take the latest version of that nifty software and its
documentation and stick it on a CD-ROM or two and mail it out to anybody 
with a subscription.  Sell the subscriptions for $100/year or so, less if 
possible. Along with the CD-ROM also send "try-it-out" PAKs for everything
on the disk. Sounds a lot like the existing CONDIST, doesn't it? 

3.  Market like mad to get people to try the stuff out, get hooked, and 
shell out the money for real PAKs.  Most people are only going to buy the
"V1.1 forever" variety, not the "V1.1 for a year", although one can even 
imagine a rental market of sorts "5 copies of DecWRITE for 4 months".
Sell hard bound documentation separately - but do allow printing of the 
online documentation.  Sell upgrades for not too much money, but sell them.
A lot of people will fork out $50 -$100 automatically for any significant 
upgrade on each piece of software they use.  That could add up to a lot of 
money.

4.  Set the prices of the PAKS at PC prices.  As we've all seen, the big 
bucks are in volume.

Assuming DEC went after this with some enthusiasm they might actually make
a dent in the low end this time - downsizing with a vengeance!  Besides, 
if you look at it, there's really only minimal work in providing the VMS 
software once you've got the PCs in place.

Consider one typical PC application: a DBMS.  At the moment I've got
Paradox on my PC at home and RDB on my VAX at work.  "Out of the box"
Paradox is vastly easier to use than RDB.  But look at DEC's catalog - they
offer both a Motif design program (for setting up a Database) and a Motif
SQL program (EasySql, for accessing the database).  I have yet to try
either one of these (too expensive), but they look like they would work
quite well.  Now I wouldn't pay DEC $8000 for this when I could get Paradox
for $400, but I would certainly consider buying RDB (with the Motif front
ends) for a price near that of Paradox.  Heresy to offer RDB at these
prices?  Maybe,  but who does it hurt?  Better to get my $500 than not sell
it to me at all. 

Similarly, Microsoft Works is nice, but I might shell out for All-In-1 if it
was priced competitively. 

Look at it another way - how many software products has Microsoft got on
hand, and how many has DEC got?  DEC's problem is that they don't seem
ready to make the effort to take all of this existing software, spruce it
up, and shove it straight down Microsoft's throat.  Even if some of the
consumer applications out there are better than DEC's, DEC could still sell
their's if they dropped the price enough.  Better to sell it low than not 
sell it at all.

Ditto for the Unix/Ultrix market with an OSF Alpha PC.  Although if DEC
speeds up POSIX a bit and get the bugs out of UCX they might even get a
slice of the Unix market with the VMS PCs. 

Enough wishful thinking for one day.

