In article <1993Feb5.170249.7099@reed.edu> Robert Ankeney, bob@reed.edu
writes:
>     We're looking at putting a high-speed modem of some kind on our
>MicroVax II.  I understand there are problems involved, and wonder what
>problems to expect.  We would be putting it on a DHV11 port, and I've
>been told the DHV isn't really quite up to handling 19200 sustained.
>Mainly, file transfers would be from the Vax to another machine.  As
>I recall, transferring to the Vax at high speeds brings it to it's
>knees (which isn't that far to go anyway :-) ).
>     If anyone can tell me what to expect and recommend a particular
>brand of (preferably low-cost) modem (I'm assuming 14.4K is doable),
>I'd sure appreciate it.  I would assume that since error correction is
>built-in, that a 14.4K would work and be able to transfer better than
>just a 9600.

I have a Telebit T2500 on my MVII via DHV11. I use it for DECUS uucp
feeds with PEP, and for remote Appletalk with AsyncServeR with v.32. The
port is configured for 19200 with hardware flow control; both types of
access cause a considerable sustained throughput. I haven't noticed the
machine grind to a halt with either. While I have a WorldBlazer at home,
I haven't put one on the MV II because it doesn't seem to be able to
sustain much higher baud rates than it does with PEP, and for protocol
file transfers, PEP with protocol spoofing gives much better performance
than v.32 or v.32bis. In my experience, v.32bis only presents an
advantage for "symmetrical" protocols (ARA, SLIP, etc.). Thus, I'd look
for a pair of used Telebits, which should be cheaper than a single decent
quality v.32bis modem, and for which it is fairly easy to get advice on
settings.

Hope this helps,

