> 128KB cache module to plug in the card.  This is high but not unreal.

  Whether or not it is "unreal" depends on your amount of brand loyalty, I
suppose. I can get a 486DX33 motherboard without the CPU chip but including
256Kb of parity-checked 20nS cache for less than $193.

> I also inquired as to the CPU upgrades available for the "Intel Xpress
> Architecture" CPU plug in slot.  $2200 for a 486DX 50Mhz!!!!  What planet
> are these creatures from?  I can buy a FULL DEC PC (monitor, disk, RAM...)
> for less than that let alone a clone!  I hate to see what a Pentium or Alpha
> CPU module will go for.

  That's insane. Given that a properly designed motherboard can go from a
486SX25 through 486DX50 and a 33/66 DX2 (the one I mentioned above will, for
example), the upgrade should not cost more than the CPU chip and crystal
themselves. You could probably buy a motherboard with the extra row of pins
to support the Pentium "Overdrive" flavor upgrade _and_ the upgrade for that
price and have a good deal of cash left over.

> This potential for upgrade was a key to my decision.  Aside from being
> stupid and not checking out the upgrade costs, what am I missing here?
> Are there third party boards out for this Xpress architecture slot?
> Did DEC quote me the wrong price?  Do I need to be sitting down to
> hear the Pentium or Alpha prices?  Am I just another sucker?  Are these
> chips made of solid gold?

  The problem with "upgradable" motherboards, especially those with daughter-
boards for CPU and/or cache, is that the vendors force you to take their up-
grade on their pricing terms. Given that you're giving up the old CPU chip
anyway, the motherboard cost should only be a few hundred dollars. Why not
just toss the whole motherboard/CPU and buy a new one - you'd be paying a lot
less. As an example, we buy the above motherboard with a 486DX33 for $540.
I believe the price for a DX50 is about $150 higher and a 33/66 DX2 is about
$350 higher. With prices like that, why get taken for an "upgradable". (Note
that some vendors understand this and will gladly sell you their name-brand
system which takes simple CPU/crystal replacement. Look in the PC magazines.

