From article <C1s76E.A71@vcd.hp.com>, by edmoore@vcd.hp.com (Ed Moore):
> I don't normally have a reason to inquire about DEC hardware, but someone I
> know was given a Dec Mate II, Model PC 278-A.

The DECmate family is based on a VLSI implementation of the PDP-8 done
by Intersil (the IM6120).  It can run essentially all PDP-8 software,
including OS/8 and ancilliary stuff like compilers.  In addition, it
could be configured with a variety of coprocessors; I believe these
included a Z-80 with CP-M support, and also an 80?86 with nonstandard
MS-DOS support.  I may be mistaken about the details of the available
coprocessors.

In any case, most of the DECmates were sold with DEC's WPS (a word
processing system), and by the end of the production run, DEC wasn't
saying much about their ability to act as anything other than dedicated
word processing machines.

I'd love to get production figures on the DECmates -- I maintain the
FAQ file for the PDP-8, and one thing I'd like to know is the total
production run of general purpose "family of 8" computers sold by DEC.
I have fairly reliable figures for production prior to 1970, when PDP-8
computers were still innovative, but the mass production really kicked
into high gear during the 70's and early 80's when huge numbers of PDP-8
E, F and M machines were sold despite the fact that they were obsolete
from a technological viewpoint the moment they left the assembly line.
The DECmates were made entirely in the 1980's, and again, they were
technologically obsolete the day they were made, but large numbers were
sold.
					Doug Jones
					jones@cs.uiowa.edu


